<h3>Data and Question Alignment (Trifecta Checkup Framework)</h3>
<p>Following the Junk Charts Trifecta Checkup framework (Fung, 2014), the alignment between the Data and the Question in this visualisation is strong. The question—"How has total household debt changed across the U.S. over the last several years?"—is well-posed. The data from the Federal Reserve Bank of New York directly tracks percentage changes in different debt categories from 2003 to 2020. The visualisation successfully answers the core question. However, it only shows aggregated national trends, which may limit a more detailed understanding.</p>

<h3>Verification Summary</h3>
<p>All values in the HowMuch.net visualisation were verified against the source data from the Federal Reserve Bank of New York's Household Debt and Credit Report. The key trends, such as the 546% increase in student loans and the 114% increase in auto loans, were confirmed. No discrepancies were identified, confirming the visualisation accurately represents the source data.</p>

<h3>Data Quality</h3>
<p>The Federal Reserve Bank of New York is a highly reputable and authoritative data source. It produces the Quarterly Report on Household Debt and Credit using rigorous, transparent methodologies. Limitations include the data only covering individuals with a credit report and providing only aggregate trends. Despite these, the source is of the highest quality for this type of analysis.</p>

